
In a bug bounty workflow, you can use Retire.js to find quick, low-hanging fruit by identifying outdated front-end components on a target's infrastructure. Many programs offer rewards for demonstrating an exploit (like Cross-Site Scripting or Prototype Pollution) stemming from an outdated library.
Here is an automated pipeline you can build right in your terminal to turn this into an efficient hunting routine.
------------------------------
## Phase 1: Create a Reusable Scanner Script
Instead of typing the local database flags every time, create a custom helper script named hunt-js in your local bin folder so you can invoke it from anywhere.

   1. Open a new script file:
   
   nano ~/hunt-js
   
   2. Paste the following logic (which automatically passes your offline jsrepository.json paths):
   
   #!/bin/bash
   TARGET_DIR=$1if [ -z "$TARGET_DIR" ]; then
       TARGET_DIR="."fi
   retire --path "$TARGET_DIR" --jsrepo /home/user/www.betpawa.cm/jsrepository.json
   
   3. Save and exit (Ctrl+O, Enter, Ctrl+X), then make it executable:
   
   chmod +x ~/hunt-js && sudo mv ~/hunt-js /usr/local/bin/hunt-js
   
   
Now you can simply navigate to any directory and type hunt-js to scan it.
------------------------------
## Phase 2: Live Bug Bounty Automation Pipeline
When testing a target company (e.g., target.com), you should couple Retire.js with automated URL extractors to find hidden JavaScript assets.
## 1. Gather all JavaScript URLs using Gau or Waymore
Don't just crawl the homepage. Query historical passive web archives to find every script file ever deployed by the target:

# Install Go-based URL miner if you don't have it
sudo apt install golang -y && go install ://github.com
# Fetch all historical JS endpoints for your target
~/go/bin/gau --mime js target.com > target_js_urls.txt

## 2. Mass-Download the Discovered Scripts
Create a fresh recon directory and fetch all discovered files instantly:

mkdir -p ~/recon/target && cd ~/recon/target
wget -i target_js_urls.txt --tries=2 --timeout=5 || true

## 3. Run the Mass Scan
Execute your optimized local engine across the entire folder of assets:

hunt-js

------------------------------
## Phase 3: Reporting to Platforms (YesWeHack / HackerOne)
If your scan flags an asset (e.g., bootstrap-3.3.7.js or jquery-1.12.4.js):

* Do not report the version number alone: Most premium platforms will instantly close your report as "Informational" or "Duplicate/Ineligible" if you only provide a static scanner screenshot.
* Build a Proof of Concept (PoC): Look at the CVE number provided by Retire.js. Look up a public exploit payload on GitHub or Exploit-DB (e.g., finding a parameter that passes data directly to a vulnerable .html() DOM manipulator).
* Verify the Context: Ensure the vulnerable script is running live on an active in-scope subdomain (like ://target.com...) and can actually execute an alert payload in a real browser session.

